{"description":"## Motivation

goldenbraid_inventory.go's ListPlasmids validation uses a pointer-based nullable pattern
(PlasmidNameContext stored as *PlasmidNameContext in PipelineContext) with multi-step Either
unwrapping. goldenbraid.go solves the same problem cleanly with O.Option + A.Head + O.Fold.
This refactor brings the inventory code to the same standard.

## Goal

Replace the pointer-nullable approach with an Option-based two-phase design:
- Phase 1: lookup → O.Option[*stock.Plasmid]
- Phase 2: O.Fold → None silently skips (nothing to do), Some runs the full RE.Bind inventory sub-pipeline

---

## What Gets Eliminated

| Symbol | Type | Reason |
|---|---|---|
| PlasmidNameContext | struct | Exists only to bundle name+resp; Option removes the need |
| PipelineContext.NameContext *PlasmidNameContext | field | Pointer-as-nullable replaced by Option |
| setPlasmidContext | function | Setter for the removed field |
| requireNameContext | function | Un-nullifies pointer; vanishes with Option |
| hasSinglePlasmid | function | len==1 check; A.Head handles empty naturally |
| plasmidCountError | function | Error for the above; no longer needed |
| firstPlasmidID | function | Extracts ID; Option carries the Plasmid directly |
| validatePlasmidResponseRE | function | All three steps above collapsed into the O.Fold |

---

## What Changes

### 1. collectionToOption — move to shared file

Currently in goldenbraid.go. Both files are package stockcenter so they share scope, but moving
to goldenbraid_common.go makes ownership clear. Create new file goldenbraid_common.go with:

```go
// collectionToOption converts PlasmidCollection to Option[Plasmid].
// Returns None if the collection has no data (plasmid not found).
func collectionToOption(
	collection *stock.PlasmidCollection,
) O.Option[*stock.Plasmid] {
	return F.Pipe2(
		collection.Data,
		A.Head[*stock.PlasmidCollection_Data], // None if empty
		O.Map(convertCollectionDataToPlasmid),
	)
}

func convertCollectionDataToPlasmid(
	data *stock.PlasmidCollection_Data,
) *stock.Plasmid {
	return &stock.Plasmid{
		Data: &stock.Plasmid_Data{
			Type:       data.Type,
			Id:         data.Id,
			Attributes: data.Attributes,
		},
	}
}
```

Remove both functions from goldenbraid.go.

---

### 2. PipelineContext — remove NameContext field

```go
// BEFORE
type PipelineContext struct {
	PlasmidName      string
	Location         string
	NameContext      *PlasmidNameContext   // <-- remove this
	PlasmidID        string
	Inventory        *pb.TaggedAnnotationGroupCollection
	InventoryDeleted bool
	AnnotationIDs    []string
	GroupCreated     bool
}

// AFTER
type PipelineContext struct {
	PlasmidName      string
	Location         string
	PlasmidID        string
	Inventory        *pb.TaggedAnnotationGroupCollection
	InventoryDeleted bool
	AnnotationIDs    []string
	GroupCreated     bool
}
```

---

### 3. listPlasmidsRE — return Option instead of PlasmidNameContext

```go
// BEFORE: Processing[PlasmidNameContext]
// AFTER:  Processing[O.Option[*stock.Plasmid]]

func listPlasmidsRE(ctx PipelineContext) Processing[O.Option[*stock.Plasmid]] {
	return F.Pipe1(
		RE.Ask[Deps, error](),
		RE.Chain(func(deps Deps) Processing[O.Option[*stock.Plasmid]] {
			return RE.FromIOEither[Deps](
				F.Pipe3(
					IOE.TryCatchError(func() (*stock.PlasmidCollection, error) {
						return deps.StockClient.ListPlasmids(
							context.Background(),
							&stock.StockParameters{
								Filter: fmt.Sprintf(
									"plasmid_name===%s",
									ctx.PlasmidName,
								),
								Limit: plasmidSearchLimit,
							},
						)
					}),
					IOE.MapLeft[*stock.PlasmidCollection](func(err error) error {
						return fmt.Errorf(
							"error listing plasmids for name %s: %w",
							ctx.PlasmidName,
							err,
						)
					}),
					IOE.Map[error](collectionToOption),
				),
			)
		}),
	)
}
```

Key change: final IOE step is IOE.Map[error](collectionToOption) — reuses the shared function.
No more PlasmidNameContext struct wrapping.

Note: F.Pipe3 because IOE.TryCatchError + IOE.MapLeft + IOE.Map = 3 steps chained on the
initial value (the TryCatchError call), making it a pipe of 2 functions on one value — actually
F.Pipe2 with the TryCatchError as the initial value and MapLeft+Map as the two functions.
Verify arity at implementation time.

---

### 4. processFoundPlasmid — new function

Extracts the RE.Bind sub-pipeline into a named function. Receives a concrete plasmidID (already
resolved from Option), constructs a fresh PipelineContext with PlasmidID pre-populated, then
runs the existing RE.Bind steps unchanged.

```go
// processFoundPlasmid runs the full inventory pipeline for a known plasmid ID.
// Called from the Some branch of O.Fold in processRowToSummary.
func processFoundPlasmid(
	deps Deps,
	record InventoryRecord,
	plasmidID string,
) E.Either[error, InventoryProcessingSummary] {
	ctx := PipelineContext{
		PlasmidName: record.PlasmidName,
		Location:    record.Location,
		PlasmidID:   plasmidID,
	}
	return F.Pipe2(
		F.Pipe5(
			RE.Of[Deps, error](ctx),
			RE.Bind(setInventory, getInventoryRE),
			RE.Bind(setInventoryDeleted, deleteInventoryIfExistsRE),
			RE.Bind(setAnnotationIDs, createInventoryAnnotationsRE),
			RE.Bind(setGroupCreated, createAnnotationGroupRE),
			RE.Chain(markInventoryExistenceRE),
		)(deps),
		fputil.ToEither[error, string],
		E.Map[error](onProcessSuccess),
	)
}
```

Type flow:
- F.Pipe5(...)(deps) applies Deps to ReaderIOEither → IOE.IOEither[error, string]
- fputil.ToEither[error, string] runs the IOEither → E.Either[error, string]
- E.Map[error](onProcessSuccess) maps string → InventoryProcessingSummary

All downstream functions (setInventory, setInventoryDeleted, setAnnotationIDs, setGroupCreated,
getInventoryRE, deleteInventoryIfExistsRE, createInventoryAnnotationsRE,
createAnnotationGroupRE, markInventoryExistenceRE) are unchanged.

---

### 5. processRowToSummary — two-phase redesign

```go
// processRowToSummary processes one inventory row and returns a summary.
// Phase 1: lookup plasmid by name → Either[error, Option[*Plasmid]]
// Phase 2: O.Fold — None skips silently, Some runs processFoundPlasmid.
func processRowToSummary(
	deps Deps,
	record InventoryRecord,
) InventoryProcessingSummary {
	return F.Pipe2(
		// Phase 1: lookup → Either[error, Option[*Plasmid]]
		fputil.ToEither(
			F.Pipe2(
				RE.Of[Deps, error](NewPipelineContext(record)),
				RE.Chain(listPlasmidsRE),
			)(deps),
		),
		// Phase 2: branch on the Option
		E.Chain(func(plasmidOpt O.Option[*stock.Plasmid]) E.Either[error, InventoryProcessingSummary] {
			return O.Fold(
				func() E.Either[error, InventoryProcessingSummary] {
					// None: plasmid not found in stock — nothing to do
					return E.Right[error](InventoryProcessingSummary{})
				},
				func(plasmid *stock.Plasmid) E.Either[error, InventoryProcessingSummary] {
					// Some: found — run full inventory pipeline
					return processFoundPlasmid(deps, record, plasmid.Data.Id)
				},
			)(plasmidOpt)
		}),
		// Final fold: errors become error summaries, success passes through
		E.Fold(
			onProcessError(record.PlasmidName),
			F.Identity[InventoryProcessingSummary],
		),
	)
}
```

The old F.Pipe9 with 9 RE.Bind steps collapses into F.Pipe2 with two clean phases.
The final fold uses F.Identity (not onProcessSuccess) because processFoundPlasmid already
returns InventoryProcessingSummary, not string.

---

## Test Changes (goldenbraid_inventory_test.go)

### TestProcessRow — no mock changes needed

The existing test mocks ListPlasmids returning one plasmid, which now hits the Some branch of
O.Fold and enters processFoundPlasmid. Mock expectations are identical — no changes to mock
setup. Only internal wiring changes.

### New test: TestProcessRowSkipsWhenPlasmidNotFound

```go
func TestProcessRowSkipsWhenPlasmidNotFound(t *testing.T) {
	mockStock := new(InventoryMockStockClient)
	mockAnno := new(InventoryMockAnnotationClient)
	deps := Deps{StockClient: mockStock, AnnotationClient: mockAnno}
	record := InventoryRecord{PlasmidName: "pMissing", Location: "Box99"}

	mockStock.On("ListPlasmids", mock.Anything, mock.Anything, mock.Anything).
		Return(&stock.PlasmidCollection{}, nil).Once()

	summary := processRowToSummary(deps, record)

	require.Equal(t, 0, summary.ErrorCount)
	require.Equal(t, 0, summary.SuccessCount)
	mockStock.AssertExpectations(t)
	mockAnno.AssertNotCalled(t, "ListAnnotationGroups")
}
```

Verifies: empty collection → O.None → silent skip → zero-value summary. The annotation client
is never called, confirming the None branch short-circuits correctly.

---

## File Changes Summary

| File | Action |
|---|---|
| goldenbraid_common.go | CREATE — collectionToOption + convertCollectionDataToPlasmid |
| goldenbraid.go | REMOVE collectionToOption + convertCollectionDataToPlasmid (moved) |
| goldenbraid_inventory.go | REMOVE 7 symbols listed above; MODIFY listPlasmidsRE + PipelineContext + processRowToSummary; ADD processFoundPlasmid |
| goldenbraid_inventory_test.go | UPDATE TestProcessRow (verify passes unchanged); ADD TestProcessRowSkipsWhenPlasmidNotFound |

---

## Semantic Clarification: None = skip, not error

The previous code treated "no plasmid found" as a Left (error) from validatePlasmidResponseRE,
surfacing as an error in the summary. With this refactor, None is an explicit, clean skip.
The InventoryProcessingSummary returned for a skipped row is the zero value —
SuccessCount=0, ErrorCount=0. The Semigroup fold absorbs it without inflating either counter.
This is the correct semantic: if the stock service has no plasmid for an inventory row,
there is nothing to annotate and it is not an error."}

